1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
It's the Queen of Bridges, dramatically spanning a mile of turbulent water.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:13,000
But its birth was a fiery mix of high-minded idealism, brilliant engineering, and daring construction work.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Now, the Golden Gate Bridge on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,640
Its grandeur is often shrouded in a creeping cocoon

5
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:35,640
of dense fog.

6
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:38,540
But when the fog lifts in San Francisco,

7
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:41,940
the Golden Gate Bridge beckons as a shining example of how

8
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:45,940
engineering and architectural design can work together to

9
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:51,060
transform functionality into artistry.

10
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:55,300
When completed in 1937, it instantly became symbolic of

11
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:58,300
the American dream. Well, certainly the Golden Gate

12
00:00:58,301 --> 00:01:02,060
Certainly the Golden Gate Bridge is a critically important piece of transportation infrastructure.

13
00:01:02,061 --> 00:01:04,740
It's also a remarkable engineering achievement.

14
00:01:04,741 --> 00:01:09,720
But I think the thing that sets it apart from other big bridges around the world is the

15
00:01:09,721 --> 00:01:13,860
love affair that the people of San Francisco, of California, even the United States have

16
00:01:13,861 --> 00:01:14,860
with this bridge.

17
00:01:14,861 --> 00:01:18,960
And our workers say that the Golden Gate Bridge is alive and that it lives and breathes.

18
00:01:18,961 --> 00:01:22,460
And when you get out there on the bridge, you find out that that's really true.

19
00:01:22,461 --> 00:01:23,460
It does.

20
00:01:23,461 --> 00:01:27,900
We estimate we have somewhere between 9 and 14 million visitors a year who either come

21
00:01:27,901 --> 00:01:31,940
ride a bicycle over it, drive over it, or just come and park

22
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:36,300
and look at it and really enjoy the experience of seeing this

23
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:38,940
modern marvel of engineering.

24
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:42,140
The bridge is a major attraction.

25
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:46,860
San Francisco's signature landmark

26
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:49,360
spans the mouth of the picturesque Golden Gate

27
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:53,500
Strait and inspires awe as the world's most majestic example

28
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:56,400
of suspension bridge design.

29
00:01:56,401 --> 00:02:03,080
What that means is you have main suspension cables that start on the land, that rise up

30
00:02:03,081 --> 00:02:07,360
over the top of a tower, drape down in mid-span, rise up over the next tower and come down

31
00:02:07,361 --> 00:02:08,800
again on land.

32
00:02:08,801 --> 00:02:13,620
On the end of each individual cable is a mass of concrete that pulls it to the ground.

33
00:02:13,621 --> 00:02:18,400
Each chunk of concrete weighs about 120 million pounds, so it's a tremendous weight to hold

34
00:02:18,401 --> 00:02:24,200
a tremendous force, because the entire weight of the bridge is hanging on those cables.

35
00:02:24,201 --> 00:02:26,700
that drape over the top of the main cables,

36
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:28,400
and those are spaced every 50 feet

37
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:29,840
along the length of the bridge.

38
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:32,200
And those suspension ropes, if you wish,

39
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:35,140
are what hold the deck up, the deck that we walk and drive

40
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:36,440
across.

41
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:38,940
San Francisco's demands as a port city

42
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,160
dictated the bridge's colossal dimensions.

43
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:43,960
During the initial planning stages,

44
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:47,060
the Navy required the clearance under the bridge at high tide

45
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:50,120
to be a minimum of 220 feet.

46
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:52,560
When first finished, the Golden Gate

47
00:02:52,561 --> 00:02:55,460
The Golden Gate was far and away the biggest suspension

48
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:57,300
bridge in the world.

49
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:00,000
The total length of the bridge, including the approaches,

50
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:03,300
is 1.7 miles.

51
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:06,000
The main suspension span between the towers

52
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:10,420
is 4,200 feet long, which was 700 feet longer than that

53
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:13,480
of the previous record holder, New York's George Washington

54
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:15,280
Bridge.

55
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:18,520
It stayed the largest bridge in the world until 1964,

56
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:22,120
so 27 years later when the Verrazano-Neros Bridge opened

57
00:03:22,121 --> 00:03:24,960
and the Verrazano Narrows Bridge is only 60 feet longer

58
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:26,120
than the Golden Gate Bridge.

59
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:28,760
So if you don't think that was on purpose,

60
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:30,800
today the Golden Gate Bridge is the eighth largest

61
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:32,000
suspension bridge in the world.

62
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,060
And some of the big bridges are truly much, much bigger.

63
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:36,700
The Akashikaikyo Bridge in Japan

64
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:38,840
is now the largest suspension bridge in the world.

65
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:42,240
And it's at 1 1⁄2 times the size of the Golden Gate Bridge.

66
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:45,640
But no bridge anywhere captures the hearts and minds

67
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:47,280
and spirits of the people who visit it

68
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:48,680
like the Golden Gate Bridge does.

69
00:03:48,681 --> 00:03:51,080
does.

70
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:52,920
Adding to the bridge's artfulness

71
00:03:53,020 --> 00:03:55,420
is the arch at its south end.

72
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:58,420
It wasn't necessary from an engineering standpoint,

73
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:00,320
but was incorporated into the design

74
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:02,920
for the sole purpose of preserving historic Fort

75
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:05,940
Point, a relic outpost from the Civil War.

76
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:08,340
It would have been cheaper to demolish the fort,

77
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,540
and there's no reason to save the fort other than respect

78
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:12,840
for those that went before us.

79
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,680
The origins of the need for the Golden Gate

80
00:04:18,681 --> 00:04:22,720
can be traced back to 1848, when gold was discovered in the hills

81
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:26,680
northeast of San Francisco. As gold fever attracted

82
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:30,120
legions of fortune hunters, the city's population soared more

83
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:36,240
than 7,000% in just two years. With bountiful wealth came all

84
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:40,340
the pleasures of the flesh. Drinking, dambling, opium, and

85
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:44,040
prostitution helped give San Francisco's waterfront, the

86
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:48,680
Barbary Coast, a reputation for hedonistic abandon.

87
00:04:48,681 --> 00:04:51,480
To puritanical America, there was a suggestion

88
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:55,980
of divine retribution when the earthquake and fire of 1906

89
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:58,620
caused destruction of biblical proportions.

90
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:07,040
But out of the ashes arose a people even more determined

91
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:10,340
to revel in the zeal of independence.

92
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:12,540
There's an attitude in San Francisco

93
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:16,200
that developed after the earthquake of 1906 that said,

94
00:05:16,201 --> 00:05:18,500
that said, you know, we've been knocked back,

95
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:20,440
but we are never going to be fallen.

96
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:22,040
And we are never going to stop.

97
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:24,200
And we're always going to be out on the cutting edge.

98
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:26,380
And this was a chance to do something that people

99
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:27,780
said couldn't be done.

100
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:32,420
And that's usually what San Francisco does best.

101
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:34,480
San Francisco rebuilt.

102
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:37,360
And soon, the streets teemed with one of the 20th century's

103
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:41,620
most life-altering innovations, the automobile.

104
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:44,060
Mass-produced motor cars allowed the common man

105
00:05:44,061 --> 00:05:46,560
to chart his own course.

106
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:49,540
The concept was all American, and weekend jaunts

107
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:52,000
quickly became a lifestyle.

108
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,940
But in San Francisco, it was becoming increasingly hard

109
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:56,740
to escape the city.

110
00:05:56,840 --> 00:05:59,800
The only way to cross the bay was by ferry.

111
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:05,680
And back in 1919, I think there were something like 123,000

112
00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:08,320
ferry car trips in a year.

113
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:12,280
By the late 20s, it was up to 2 and 1 half million.

114
00:06:12,281 --> 00:06:13,940
I mean, it was a tremendous growth.

115
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:15,980
Bottom line is traffic congestion

116
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:17,720
is not a new phenomenon.

117
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:21,520
It occurred here a very long time ago.

118
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:24,720
In the spring of 1919, San Francisco

119
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:28,520
witnessed one of the largest traffic jams in history.

120
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:30,740
An overwhelming flood of cars converged,

121
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:34,040
waiting for ferry service, triggering delays up to 18

122
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:35,540
hours long.

123
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:38,780
The only alternative was to circumvent San Francisco Bay

124
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:41,580
with a very long drive.

125
00:06:41,581 --> 00:06:44,680
If you wanted to drive to the North Bay,

126
00:06:44,780 --> 00:06:47,380
you literally had to drive from San Francisco

127
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:50,820
all the way down to San Jose, then back up on the east side

128
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:52,520
and over across the top.

129
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:55,520
Today, with all of our freeways, on a good day

130
00:06:55,620 --> 00:06:57,620
with very little traffic, that's still

131
00:06:57,720 --> 00:07:00,940
going to take you about 2 1⁄2 to 3 hours.

132
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:05,540
Back in 1920, 1925, that must have been an all-day trip,

133
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:06,940
if not longer.

134
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:09,540
And so the ferries were tremendously important.

135
00:07:11,580 --> 00:07:14,780
Until the 1920s, people generally accepted that it

136
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:18,520
would be impossible ever to bridge the Golden Gate.

137
00:07:18,620 --> 00:07:21,820
But the area's growth finally demanded that the impossible at

138
00:07:21,920 --> 00:07:25,320
least be attempted. The city turned to Joseph Strauss,

139
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:28,260
a renowned bridge engineer who reveled in accepting

140
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:32,160
monumental challenges. Strauss' peers considered

141
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:36,280
him arrogant, short-tempered, and perhaps the most brilliant

142
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:40,280
bully of his time. But he also had a solid record

143
00:07:40,281 --> 00:07:44,780
record of success. Strauss had built bridges for San

144
00:07:44,781 --> 00:07:48,280
Francisco in the past, like the Fourth Street Bridge.

145
00:07:48,281 --> 00:07:52,280
His work was structurally sound, but notoriously unattractive.

146
00:07:52,281 --> 00:07:55,280
After three years of computations and research,

147
00:07:55,281 --> 00:07:59,280
Strauss came up with a master plan, a large lumbering

148
00:07:59,281 --> 00:08:04,280
monstrosity with little beauty or practicality.

149
00:08:04,281 --> 00:08:08,280
Reaction was universally unenthusiastic, but he was hungry

150
00:08:08,281 --> 00:08:10,920
But he was hungry for the prestige a Golden Gate Bridge

151
00:08:10,921 --> 00:08:12,520
would bestow.

152
00:08:12,521 --> 00:08:14,660
Joining forces with a quickly organized team

153
00:08:14,661 --> 00:08:18,260
of academic engineers, Strauss submitted new designs

154
00:08:18,261 --> 00:08:21,700
based on the suspension bridge concept.

155
00:08:21,701 --> 00:08:23,560
The design finally selected offered

156
00:08:23,561 --> 00:08:27,100
unusual architectural beauty, along with a solution

157
00:08:27,101 --> 00:08:30,060
to the Bay Area's traffic problem.

158
00:08:30,061 --> 00:08:32,640
But for those who profited from the ferries,

159
00:08:32,641 --> 00:08:35,980
the proposed bridge represented an enormous threat.

160
00:08:35,981 --> 00:08:38,040
The Southern Pacific Railroad Company

161
00:08:38,041 --> 00:08:40,240
owned the ferries, basically.

162
00:08:40,241 --> 00:08:42,540
And they had bought up all the franchises,

163
00:08:42,541 --> 00:08:44,940
and they were making money hand over fist.

164
00:08:44,941 --> 00:08:49,080
So they certainly didn't want any bridges built.

165
00:08:49,081 --> 00:08:53,820
Southern Pacific mounted over 2,300 legal disputes.

166
00:08:53,821 --> 00:08:56,060
The Bay Area's enraged citizens countered

167
00:08:56,061 --> 00:08:59,360
with an area-wide boycott of the ferry system

168
00:08:59,361 --> 00:09:04,440
and forced Southern Pacific to drop its nuisance lawsuits.

169
00:09:04,441 --> 00:09:06,600
But there was another, more powerful force,

170
00:09:06,601 --> 00:09:07,840
also blocking construction.

171
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:10,640
In the early days before the bridge was built,

172
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:13,840
the Golden Gate Bridge District negotiated for seven years

173
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:16,140
with the Department of War at the time

174
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:18,480
over whether there should be a bridge across the entrance

175
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:20,180
to San Francisco Bay, the fear, of course,

176
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:22,980
being that an enemy could come in and bomb the bridge,

177
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:26,420
drop it into the water, and lock the Pacific Fleet

178
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:28,960
in San Francisco Bay and to halt commerce in and out

179
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:32,620
of San Francisco Bay.

180
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:36,600
The War Department eventually gave in.

181
00:09:36,601 --> 00:09:41,440
But in 1929, nine years after the bridge was first proposed,

182
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:44,900
America collapsed into the Great Depression.

183
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,140
There was barely enough money for food, let alone funds,

184
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:50,680
for the government to finance the largest bridge project

185
00:09:50,780 --> 00:09:52,920
in human history.

186
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:58,360
The proposed cost was a then staggering $35 million.

187
00:09:58,460 --> 00:10:00,360
Civic leaders put the fate of the bridge

188
00:10:00,460 --> 00:10:02,100
in the hands of the people.

189
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:04,500
What happened is that they went out to the voters

190
00:10:04,501 --> 00:10:07,240
in all of the counties from San Francisco north

191
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:09,640
to the Northern California border and said,

192
00:10:09,740 --> 00:10:12,400
would you be willing to basically put a lien

193
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:15,640
against your personal property, your farm, your vineyard,

194
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:19,140
your home, your business for the security

195
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:22,060
against these bonds so that if something happens,

196
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:25,660
we build the bridge, it doesn't stand, it falls down,

197
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:28,600
and we still owe money, we're going to come back at you

198
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:29,960
and at your house.

199
00:10:30,060 --> 00:10:31,800
And people said, yes.

200
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:34,500
I can't believe that today that people would live

201
00:10:34,501 --> 00:10:38,500
put that kind of faith in an idea.

202
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:41,600
The Bay Area's civic pride was contagious.

203
00:10:41,700 --> 00:10:44,040
Even financial institutions were ready to invest

204
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:46,440
in San Francisco's future.

205
00:10:46,540 --> 00:10:49,840
A.P. Giannini, the Bank of America founder,

206
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:51,860
felt that as a result of the Depression,

207
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:53,660
jobs were necessary.

208
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:56,460
And he offered a $5 million line of credit

209
00:10:56,560 --> 00:11:00,260
to go ahead and begin construction of the bridge.

210
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:02,160
With no government support,

211
00:11:02,161 --> 00:11:05,860
the people of San Francisco set out to demonstrate how self

212
00:11:05,861 --> 00:11:09,100
determination and self reliance could change the world.

213
00:11:11,700 --> 00:11:17,100
January 1933, on a barren finger of land by turbulent

214
00:11:17,101 --> 00:11:20,320
waters, the president of the Golden Gate Bridge District,

215
00:11:20,321 --> 00:11:24,720
William P. Filma, and Mayor Angelo Rossi of San Francisco

216
00:11:24,721 --> 00:11:28,320
ceremoniously began construction.

217
00:11:28,321 --> 00:11:31,760
For the next four years, innovative engineers and daring

218
00:11:31,761 --> 00:11:36,100
construction workers would face bad weather, a country mired in

219
00:11:36,101 --> 00:11:39,700
economic strife, and sometimes deadly accidents.

220
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:47,300
In 2003, it was estimated that the cost to rebuild the Golden

221
00:11:47,301 --> 00:11:52,320
Gate Bridge would be at least $1.2 billion, nearly 35 times

222
00:11:52,321 --> 00:11:55,620
the original construction cost of $35 million.

223
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:08,760
From vantage points all over the San Francisco area,

224
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:10,760
the Golden Gate Bridge's mighty towers

225
00:12:10,860 --> 00:12:14,700
can be seen rising majestically above the entrance to the bay.

226
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:18,720
Each steel tower weighs a hefty 22,000 tons.

227
00:12:18,820 --> 00:12:21,720
But despite the need for such mammoth support structures,

228
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:23,720
the aim of the architects was to infuse them

229
00:12:23,820 --> 00:12:25,220
with a sense of elegance.

230
00:12:25,320 --> 00:12:26,720
Part of the aesthetics of the bridge

231
00:12:26,820 --> 00:12:29,660
is how the towers are stepped or tapered as they rise up.

232
00:12:29,661 --> 00:12:33,160
up, and also the struts that cross between the two tower

233
00:12:33,260 --> 00:12:36,500
legs as they rise up to get closer together.

234
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:40,640
The tower is 746 feet tall, and all the weight of the bridge

235
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:41,940
rests on those towers.

236
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:43,740
The main cable goes up over the towers,

237
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:45,980
and those towers are in compression,

238
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:48,240
pressing straight down with the entire weight of the bridge.

239
00:12:48,340 --> 00:12:53,420
So they're one of the main parts of a suspension bridge.

240
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:56,380
In 1933, when construction commenced,

241
00:12:56,480 --> 00:12:58,660
the creation of the two immense towers

242
00:12:58,661 --> 00:13:02,660
was the engineering team's first daunting challenge.

243
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:05,060
The North Tower, partially built on dry land,

244
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:07,400
was a relatively easy task.

245
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:10,100
But creating the foundation for the South Tower

246
00:13:10,200 --> 00:13:12,000
was another story.

247
00:13:12,100 --> 00:13:14,000
It was a Herculean challenge.

248
00:13:14,100 --> 00:13:16,920
The South Tower is located 1,100 feet offshore

249
00:13:17,020 --> 00:13:18,320
in deep water.

250
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:20,020
If you look at a map of California,

251
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:22,320
about half of it drains out this opening.

252
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:25,920
The waterfalls in Yosemite, Shasta Dam, the rainfall in

253
00:13:26,020 --> 00:13:28,660
Bakersfield, it all goes out this opening year.

254
00:13:28,661 --> 00:13:32,000
The water moves very quickly, so it's a very challenging site.

255
00:13:32,100 --> 00:13:33,500
So to construct the south tower, they

256
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:37,540
built a temporary pier or trestle 1,100 feet out.

257
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:40,440
The trestle's location, so far into the open waters

258
00:13:40,540 --> 00:13:43,040
of the Golden Gate, made it a vulnerable target

259
00:13:43,140 --> 00:13:44,840
for disaster.

260
00:13:44,940 --> 00:13:47,040
Twice they lost that temporary trestle.

261
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:49,720
Once in the heavy fog, a steamer ran into the trestle

262
00:13:49,820 --> 00:13:52,020
and took out approximately 300 feet of it.

263
00:13:52,120 --> 00:13:53,620
Another time, a storm came through,

264
00:13:53,720 --> 00:13:56,420
and that storm wiped out about 800 feet of the trestle,

265
00:13:56,520 --> 00:13:58,660
almost all of it.

266
00:13:58,661 --> 00:14:02,900
The twin disasters caused over 10 months of hard labor

267
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,400
to repair.

268
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:07,460
As the project continued, designer Joseph Strauss

269
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:10,240
made sure that the drama of the bridge's construction

270
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:13,080
was played out before film and newsreel cameras.

271
00:14:13,180 --> 00:14:14,840
And I think that was for two reasons.

272
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:18,320
One is that it was a tremendous engineering feat,

273
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:21,380
and it was an interesting project to watch.

274
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:24,280
But on the other hand, we also had a chief engineer,

275
00:14:24,380 --> 00:14:27,320
Joseph Strauss, who understood the importance

276
00:14:27,321 --> 00:14:31,320
of public relations, and he understood the power of the media

277
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:33,420
to get people excited about a project.

278
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:36,500
So he was very open to having coverage.

279
00:14:36,600 --> 00:14:40,940
Newsreels covered every aspect of progress on the bridge.

280
00:14:41,040 --> 00:14:43,160
The cameras captured how explosives were used

281
00:14:43,260 --> 00:14:45,640
to excavate the bottom of the bay to prepare

282
00:14:45,740 --> 00:14:50,280
for the construction of the pier for the South Towers Foundation.

283
00:14:50,380 --> 00:14:52,920
This was done by the pilot bomb method.

284
00:14:53,020 --> 00:14:55,920
Small pilot bombs were inserted in the guide tube,

285
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:57,320
carefully set the ground.

286
00:14:57,321 --> 00:14:59,120
over the spot desired.

287
00:14:59,220 --> 00:15:02,720
They were then dropped through this guide tube and fired.

288
00:15:02,820 --> 00:15:04,920
These small charges created the holes

289
00:15:05,020 --> 00:15:06,860
to place larger charges.

290
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,960
After the explosions, seagulls would immediately swarm

291
00:15:10,060 --> 00:15:13,060
into feast on the stunned fish.

292
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:16,480
Once a series of charges had been safely detonated,

293
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:19,780
dredgers went to work to remove the loosened rock.

294
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:21,380
Once enough rock had been cleared,

295
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:23,780
workers began construction on the pier.

296
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:25,780
They had to pour the concrete underwater,

297
00:15:25,781 --> 00:15:28,980
And they did this in the 1930s where it was not commonplace.

298
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:31,180
Once they had the moat fully enclosed,

299
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:34,620
they poured concrete underwater the first about 40 feet

300
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:35,720
within the moat.

301
00:15:35,820 --> 00:15:37,860
Then they pumped the water out, and the last part

302
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:40,820
of the concrete pedestal was constructed in the dry out

303
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:44,240
in the middle of the opening that drained San Francisco Bay.

304
00:15:44,340 --> 00:15:45,960
The creation of the South Pier

305
00:15:46,060 --> 00:15:49,200
demanded split-second timing of material delivery

306
00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:51,600
on an unheard of scale.

307
00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:53,700
Mountains of sand, cement, and gravel

308
00:15:53,701 --> 00:15:56,940
and gravel were floated to the construction site in a nonstop

309
00:15:56,941 --> 00:16:01,800
armada of barges, then offloaded into enormous concrete mixing

310
00:16:01,801 --> 00:16:06,640
facilities built exclusively for the bridge project.

311
00:16:06,641 --> 00:16:09,460
Another enormous undertaking was the construction of the massive

312
00:16:09,461 --> 00:16:12,540
anchorages to which the bridge's suspension cables would be

313
00:16:12,541 --> 00:16:13,241
grounded.

314
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:19,600
Mixed concrete was pumped directly to the locations

315
00:16:19,601 --> 00:16:23,600
through movable chutes. Workers then packed and vibrated

316
00:16:23,601 --> 00:16:26,600
the thick sludge to remove hair pockets in the 12-foot thick

317
00:16:26,601 --> 00:16:30,700
blocks, laced with steel reinforcing rods.

318
00:16:30,701 --> 00:16:32,900
The work was brutal and relentless,

319
00:16:32,901 --> 00:16:36,000
because once the pouring had begun, work couldn't stop,

320
00:16:36,001 --> 00:16:39,600
or the materials would set improperly.

321
00:16:39,601 --> 00:16:42,900
Every hour, 120 cubic yards of concrete

322
00:16:42,901 --> 00:16:46,600
were pumped into a twisted maze of reinforcing steel.

323
00:16:46,601 --> 00:16:50,500
Three shifts every 24 hours under searing sun by day,

324
00:16:50,501 --> 00:16:53,940
and the crackling glare of floodlights by night.

325
00:16:54,040 --> 00:16:56,500
Still, all this work was just preparation

326
00:16:56,600 --> 00:16:59,240
for the big job ahead.

327
00:16:59,340 --> 00:17:01,880
On the other side of the North American continent,

328
00:17:01,980 --> 00:17:03,880
the belching infernos of steel mills

329
00:17:03,980 --> 00:17:07,320
were forging the body of the bridge.

330
00:17:07,420 --> 00:17:11,360
73,000 tons of steel composed the structure.

331
00:17:11,460 --> 00:17:14,160
The huge girders that would support the North Tower

332
00:17:14,260 --> 00:17:19,020
were embedded in 98 million pounds of concrete.

333
00:17:19,021 --> 00:17:22,960
Massive panels were assembled at the site into 5,000 cells

334
00:17:23,060 --> 00:17:25,460
that formed the body of the towers.

335
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:30,360
Then 600,000 rivets were used to connect the pieces.

336
00:17:30,460 --> 00:17:33,260
Rivets were the fastener of choice in the 1930s.

337
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:35,500
They heated up these steel members.

338
00:17:35,600 --> 00:17:36,980
They would throw them.

339
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:38,680
Someone would catch them with a mitt.

340
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:40,280
They'd put them in place, and they then

341
00:17:40,380 --> 00:17:43,220
would beat on the end of the rivet to form the head,

342
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:45,220
kind of like the head of a bolt or a nut.

343
00:17:45,320 --> 00:17:48,780
So while by today's standard, it may seem rather inefficient

344
00:17:48,781 --> 00:17:52,960
and cumbersome, it was state of the art in the 1930s.

345
00:17:53,060 --> 00:17:54,460
Some of the riveters, however,

346
00:17:54,560 --> 00:17:57,020
were becoming afflicted with mysterious and troubling

347
00:17:57,120 --> 00:17:59,260
ailments, referred to collectively

348
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:02,520
as Golden Gate disease.

349
00:18:02,620 --> 00:18:05,400
The symptoms included hair and tooth loss

350
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:08,400
and disorientation and instability.

351
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:10,640
Chemists soon discovered that when the hot rivets made

352
00:18:10,740 --> 00:18:13,340
contact with the primer paint on the steel,

353
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:15,580
toxic vapors were emitted and inhaled

354
00:18:15,680 --> 00:18:18,780
by the unsuspecting workers.

355
00:18:18,781 --> 00:18:22,620
Respirators quickly became standard equipment.

356
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:25,020
But even under such dangerous conditions,

357
00:18:25,120 --> 00:18:28,120
work moved fast, often ahead of schedule,

358
00:18:28,220 --> 00:18:30,520
thanks to the competitive spirit of the men.

359
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:35,300
You have to be quick and strong, a lot of guts,

360
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:39,360
climb like a monkey, and be as agile as a cat.

361
00:18:39,460 --> 00:18:44,300
I'd be scared of nothing, work hard, drink like hell,

362
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:46,680
take care of all the women, all the Lucy's.

363
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:51,180
Oh, they loved us.

364
00:18:51,280 --> 00:18:54,220
Workers on the bridge earned up to $11 a day,

365
00:18:54,320 --> 00:18:58,220
a handsome salary in 1933.

366
00:18:58,320 --> 00:19:01,020
These were highly coveted jobs while the country

367
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:03,020
was in the depths of the Depression,

368
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:04,920
and men were hungry for work.

369
00:19:05,020 --> 00:19:08,340
I'm told stories by people who are still alive today

370
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:10,640
that worked on the construction of the bridge

371
00:19:10,740 --> 00:19:14,440
that they can remember being at work in the afternoons,

372
00:19:14,540 --> 00:19:16,940
and the winds would be really blowing,

373
00:19:16,941 --> 00:19:19,680
and they'd be tied off so that they'd be safe.

374
00:19:19,780 --> 00:19:23,040
But they could also smell, wafting up from below them,

375
00:19:23,140 --> 00:19:27,220
the smell of beans cooking in coffee cans from all of the men

376
00:19:27,320 --> 00:19:29,980
who had no jobs and were literally cooking their dinner

377
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:33,860
down below the bridge waiting for some guy up overhead

378
00:19:33,960 --> 00:19:35,920
to fall and lose his job.

379
00:19:38,760 --> 00:19:43,360
In 1934, the longshoremen who worked San Francisco's port

380
00:19:43,460 --> 00:19:45,660
decided to strike and included demands

381
00:19:45,661 --> 00:19:48,900
that they be afforded the same wages and benefits as the men

382
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,400
on the Golden Gate Bridge.

383
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:54,460
Violence erupted, and the government took a hard line

384
00:19:54,560 --> 00:19:56,900
against the strikers.

385
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,100
The public was alarmed by the attacks on the dock workers.

386
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:02,820
In an unprecedented show of solidarity,

387
00:20:02,920 --> 00:20:05,720
the city went on a general strike.

388
00:20:05,820 --> 00:20:09,720
For almost four full days, all work stopped in San Francisco,

389
00:20:09,820 --> 00:20:12,160
from elevator operators, restaurant workers,

390
00:20:12,161 --> 00:20:16,060
workers and office personnel to the men who were hanging steel

391
00:20:16,061 --> 00:20:22,060
on the Golden Gate Bridge. The National Guard was called

392
00:20:22,061 --> 00:20:26,600
out, and heavy weapons were aimed at civilians.

393
00:20:26,601 --> 00:20:30,620
Finally, before further bloodshed, management agreed to

394
00:20:30,621 --> 00:20:34,320
union negotiations, and the strike ended.

395
00:20:34,321 --> 00:20:38,060
The defiance exhibited in San Francisco resonated across the

396
00:20:38,061 --> 00:20:42,300
United States as work resumed on the bridge that many still

397
00:20:42,301 --> 00:20:44,800
believed was an impossible dream.

398
00:20:47,800 --> 00:20:50,500
The cement used for the piers and anchorages of the Golden

399
00:20:50,501 --> 00:20:53,700
Gate Bridge would be enough to build a five-foot wide sidewalk

400
00:20:53,701 --> 00:20:56,320
from San Francisco to New York.

401
00:20:56,321 --> 00:21:08,360
Thanks to its elegant design, the natural beauty of its

402
00:21:08,361 --> 00:21:12,360
surroundings, and its striking red-orange color, the Golden

403
00:21:12,361 --> 00:21:15,280
Gate Bridge is one of the most photographed landmarks in the

404
00:21:15,281 --> 00:21:16,280
world.

405
00:21:21,280 --> 00:21:24,780
Maintaining the bridge's picture postcard looks is not just a

406
00:21:24,781 --> 00:21:28,280
of aesthetics, but also of necessity.

407
00:21:28,380 --> 00:21:30,520
The key to everything in maintaining a bridge

408
00:21:30,620 --> 00:21:31,780
is keeping it painted.

409
00:21:31,880 --> 00:21:33,720
The Golden Gate Bridge is in probably one

410
00:21:33,820 --> 00:21:35,820
of the harshest environments of any bridge in the world.

411
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:38,120
And if you're going to compare it to maintaining anything else,

412
00:21:38,220 --> 00:21:40,160
it's probably most comparable to maintaining

413
00:21:40,260 --> 00:21:41,960
offshore oil platforms.

414
00:21:42,060 --> 00:21:44,940
We have an awful lot of salty fog air here

415
00:21:45,040 --> 00:21:49,580
and an awful lot of wind and just an awful lot of bad weather

416
00:21:49,680 --> 00:21:50,580
in general.

417
00:21:50,680 --> 00:21:53,680
It's a steel bridge, and it's subject to corrosion, rust,

418
00:21:53,681 --> 00:21:56,680
And in order to prevent that, you need to keep a good coat

419
00:21:56,681 --> 00:21:58,680
of paint on it.

420
00:21:58,681 --> 00:22:00,680
Protecting the bridge from the elements includes the

421
00:22:00,681 --> 00:22:04,680
precarious job of painting the main cables strung high on the

422
00:22:04,681 --> 00:22:06,680
bridge over the bay.

423
00:22:06,681 --> 00:22:10,680
When you're walking down, the cable is only a...

424
00:22:10,681 --> 00:22:12,680
It's a three-foot diameter cable.

425
00:22:12,681 --> 00:22:15,680
Of course, you only get to walk on the top foot of it, you know,

426
00:22:15,681 --> 00:22:18,680
and it's rounded, which makes it a little interesting, to say

427
00:22:18,681 --> 00:22:19,680
the least.

428
00:22:19,681 --> 00:22:22,980
You actually have a cable on each side of you

429
00:22:22,981 --> 00:22:26,420
to hook into with your lanyards and stuff.

430
00:22:26,560 --> 00:22:27,560
I mean, you have to be up there

431
00:22:27,561 --> 00:22:29,880
with all your safety gear on and everything like that.

432
00:22:29,940 --> 00:22:31,960
It's not like you go up there

433
00:22:31,961 --> 00:22:34,100
and, you know, like you're walking down a sidewalk.

434
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:37,200
Main cables being one of the most important elements

435
00:22:37,201 --> 00:22:38,780
of the bridge, you don't want to get moisture

436
00:22:38,781 --> 00:22:41,460
inside that cable and have any kind of corrosion starting.

437
00:22:42,120 --> 00:22:43,660
And so they're up there constantly

438
00:22:43,661 --> 00:22:45,900
looking for spots where the paint has broken down

439
00:22:45,901 --> 00:22:47,420
and repairing those spots.

440
00:22:47,421 --> 00:22:50,420
Painting the bridge is a full-time operation.

441
00:22:50,520 --> 00:22:53,320
There is a rumor that we paint it from one end

442
00:22:53,420 --> 00:22:55,920
to the other end, and then we turn around and repaint it

443
00:22:56,020 --> 00:22:57,360
back in another direction.

444
00:22:57,460 --> 00:22:59,360
That's not actually how it's done.

445
00:22:59,460 --> 00:23:01,260
We paint based on where it's needed.

446
00:23:01,360 --> 00:23:04,180
One of the things that we're very actively involved in

447
00:23:04,280 --> 00:23:07,180
is always looking for new paint technologies

448
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:10,680
to improve the consistency and the quality of the materials

449
00:23:10,780 --> 00:23:14,220
that are used to make the job easier to do.

450
00:23:14,320 --> 00:23:15,780
The actual color of this bridge,

451
00:23:15,781 --> 00:23:17,780
The total color of this bridge, which is known as

452
00:23:17,781 --> 00:23:19,780
International Orange, was not selected

453
00:23:19,781 --> 00:23:21,780
until construction was underway.

454
00:23:21,781 --> 00:23:23,780
Different people had opinions, and back in the 30s,

455
00:23:23,781 --> 00:23:25,780
it's no different than today.

456
00:23:25,781 --> 00:23:27,780
People shared or voiced their opinions.

457
00:23:27,781 --> 00:23:29,780
Some people thought the towers should be black,

458
00:23:29,781 --> 00:23:31,780
so they would disappear or blend in, which is rather ominous

459
00:23:31,781 --> 00:23:33,780
when you think about it today.

460
00:23:33,781 --> 00:23:35,780
Others suggested gray.

461
00:23:37,780 --> 00:23:39,780
However, the Navy had very strong opinions.

462
00:23:39,781 --> 00:23:42,780
They felt that that would not be visible enough

463
00:23:42,781 --> 00:23:45,780
in the gray fog that we have, and they were pushing

464
00:23:45,781 --> 00:23:48,680
painted black and yellow stripes.

465
00:23:48,780 --> 00:23:52,580
And all I can say is thank goodness that Irving Morrow,

466
00:23:52,680 --> 00:23:55,620
who was one of the design architects on the bridge,

467
00:23:55,720 --> 00:23:58,260
just put his foot down and kept saying,

468
00:23:58,360 --> 00:24:02,360
no, we need to have something else.

469
00:24:02,460 --> 00:24:04,900
As it turned out, the answer materialized

470
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,400
right in front of the bridge's designers

471
00:24:07,500 --> 00:24:10,280
in the form of the primer paint used to protect the steel

472
00:24:10,380 --> 00:24:13,100
parts waiting for assembly.

473
00:24:13,200 --> 00:24:15,040
The original paint came from Pennsylvania.

474
00:24:15,041 --> 00:24:17,340
it had a shock primer on it to protect the steel

475
00:24:17,341 --> 00:24:19,080
from corrosion of red lead.

476
00:24:19,081 --> 00:24:22,840
As the red lead aged, it gave it this burnt orange color.

477
00:24:22,841 --> 00:24:24,080
And as they were constructing the bridge,

478
00:24:24,081 --> 00:24:26,080
people started looking at the bridge and saying,

479
00:24:26,081 --> 00:24:27,080
that's a neat color.

480
00:24:27,081 --> 00:24:28,080
It fits.

481
00:24:28,081 --> 00:24:29,580
It blends in with the hills.

482
00:24:29,581 --> 00:24:31,800
And so when it was all said and done,

483
00:24:31,801 --> 00:24:33,700
the Navy bought into the idea

484
00:24:33,701 --> 00:24:36,000
that an international orange bridge

485
00:24:36,001 --> 00:24:39,200
would be visible enough, even in a dark fog,

486
00:24:39,201 --> 00:24:44,000
for their ships to be able to see and to go around.

487
00:24:44,001 --> 00:24:47,200
In one sense, the Golden Gate Bridge's strong and vibrant

488
00:24:47,300 --> 00:24:51,040
hue reflects the colorful personalities who often risk

489
00:24:51,140 --> 00:24:53,940
their lives to help make the bridge a reality.

490
00:24:54,040 --> 00:24:57,640
You know, ironworkers are larger than life.

491
00:24:57,740 --> 00:24:59,740
They have a tremendously challenging job.

492
00:24:59,840 --> 00:25:02,760
One such ironworker here in the Bay Area is named Al Zampa.

493
00:25:02,860 --> 00:25:04,560
He's since passed away a couple of years ago.

494
00:25:04,660 --> 00:25:07,060
And Al's famous because he's a member of the Halfway to Hell

495
00:25:07,160 --> 00:25:07,960
Club.

496
00:25:08,060 --> 00:25:10,760
He fell one day and survived.

497
00:25:10,860 --> 00:25:12,560
I was the youngest.

498
00:25:12,561 --> 00:25:16,700
Everybody there was amazed that I could do that work.

499
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:18,240
You know?

500
00:25:18,340 --> 00:25:19,800
Most guys learn.

501
00:25:19,900 --> 00:25:22,740
And they try it, but they couldn't make it.

502
00:25:22,840 --> 00:25:23,740
They got scared.

503
00:25:26,580 --> 00:25:29,320
Like all men who work at Lofty Heights,

504
00:25:29,420 --> 00:25:32,140
Zampa understood that the possibility of sudden death

505
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:34,580
was part of the job.

506
00:25:34,680 --> 00:25:38,260
A careless slip could send a worker plummeting to his doom,

507
00:25:38,360 --> 00:25:41,560
slamming his body against the surface of the water below,

508
00:25:41,561 --> 00:25:45,400
with the impact of 15,000 pounds per square inch,

509
00:25:45,500 --> 00:25:50,540
a force equal to a car hitting a brick wall at 80 miles an hour.

510
00:25:50,640 --> 00:25:53,340
Unlike some other projects in the 1930s,

511
00:25:53,440 --> 00:25:56,180
when labor was cheap and easily replaceable,

512
00:25:56,280 --> 00:25:58,780
safety was a priority at the bridge site.

513
00:25:58,880 --> 00:26:00,840
They wanted to have a legacy of safety.

514
00:26:00,940 --> 00:26:03,320
So this bridge required hard hats for all workers.

515
00:26:03,420 --> 00:26:05,080
It was the first such project.

516
00:26:05,180 --> 00:26:08,080
They developed eye goggles for the workers, special creams

517
00:26:08,081 --> 00:26:11,080
to protect against the inclement weather.

518
00:26:11,081 --> 00:26:13,080
But one of the things that made it unique

519
00:26:13,081 --> 00:26:17,080
is they spent $130,000 in the midst of a Great Depression

520
00:26:17,081 --> 00:26:20,080
on a tremendous safety net to protect the workers,

521
00:26:20,081 --> 00:26:23,080
so the workers would feel safe, and so that if they fell,

522
00:26:23,081 --> 00:26:24,080
they would be saved.

523
00:26:24,081 --> 00:26:26,080
We never had a net before.

524
00:26:26,081 --> 00:26:27,080
It was the first time we used a net.

525
00:26:27,081 --> 00:26:29,080
It was just like night and day.

526
00:26:29,081 --> 00:26:31,080
I mean, guys would dance around for around.

527
00:26:31,081 --> 00:26:33,080
If they'd catch them, they'd fire, you know?

528
00:26:33,081 --> 00:26:36,080
We'd jump from one beam to the other.

529
00:26:36,081 --> 00:26:38,720
I worked up in the steel and didn't realize many times

530
00:26:38,820 --> 00:26:41,520
I wasn't even up there.

531
00:26:41,620 --> 00:26:44,820
That's how relaxed I was.

532
00:26:44,920 --> 00:26:48,120
The net was credited with saving 19 lives

533
00:26:48,220 --> 00:26:52,260
and offered psychological reassurance to most of the men.

534
00:26:52,360 --> 00:26:57,500
But on October 20, 1936, Al Zampa learned the hard way

535
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:00,840
that the protection didn't quite match up to the promise.

536
00:27:00,940 --> 00:27:03,200
He fell from an area of the North Tower

537
00:27:03,201 --> 00:27:07,100
tower where the slack fabric was improperly suspended over hard ground.

538
00:27:08,840 --> 00:27:11,520
Well, when I went down, I wasn't scared.

539
00:27:11,620 --> 00:27:13,180
I figured the net's going to catch me.

540
00:27:13,760 --> 00:27:15,400
You know where the net was on the ground.

541
00:27:16,220 --> 00:27:18,020
And you know, pull it up tight, you know.

542
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:19,380
And I hit the net.

543
00:27:19,600 --> 00:27:21,720
I flipped three times, you know.

544
00:27:22,180 --> 00:27:24,660
And I came down and flat on my back.

545
00:27:25,420 --> 00:27:26,560
Cracked four vertebrae.

546
00:27:26,660 --> 00:27:27,940
Hell, I would have killed another guy.

547
00:27:29,800 --> 00:27:31,580
And never even knocked me out.

548
00:27:33,200 --> 00:27:35,840
After 12 weeks frozen in traction,

549
00:27:35,940 --> 00:27:38,780
Zampa had to learn to walk again like an infant taking

550
00:27:38,880 --> 00:27:40,880
his first tentative steps.

551
00:27:40,980 --> 00:27:43,140
His miraculous survival attracted the attention

552
00:27:43,240 --> 00:27:44,920
of the tabloids.

553
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:47,680
They suggested a name for Zampa and all the workers

554
00:27:47,780 --> 00:27:48,980
saved by the net.

555
00:27:49,080 --> 00:27:52,120
They officially became known as the Halfway to Hell Club.

556
00:27:55,720 --> 00:27:58,320
Building a bridge is always dangerous work,

557
00:27:58,420 --> 00:28:00,800
and accidents like Al Zampa's had been factored

558
00:28:00,801 --> 00:28:03,540
into the economic equation.

559
00:28:03,640 --> 00:28:04,840
The rule of thumb is that you're

560
00:28:04,940 --> 00:28:08,000
going to lose a person's life for every million dollars

561
00:28:08,100 --> 00:28:09,240
of construction.

562
00:28:09,340 --> 00:28:11,820
And in this particular case, a $35 million bridge

563
00:28:11,920 --> 00:28:13,920
meant you were going to lose 35 people,

564
00:28:14,020 --> 00:28:15,740
according to that rule of thumb.

565
00:28:15,840 --> 00:28:19,360
Well, up until February of 1937, they literally

566
00:28:19,460 --> 00:28:21,820
had only lost one individual's life

567
00:28:21,920 --> 00:28:24,220
as a result of the construction of the bridge.

568
00:28:24,320 --> 00:28:27,700
Unfortunately, in one day, there was a horrible accident

569
00:28:27,800 --> 00:28:29,360
and they lost 10 people.

570
00:28:29,361 --> 00:28:30,860
It was nearing the end of completion,

571
00:28:30,960 --> 00:28:33,300
within six months of completion, when unfortunately,

572
00:28:33,400 --> 00:28:35,300
a paving machine fell into the net,

573
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:37,500
and it carried 10 workers to their death.

574
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:39,900
It was a tremendous tragedy.

575
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:41,600
Despite this calamitous accident

576
00:28:41,700 --> 00:28:45,840
on February 17, 1937, the bridge's overall safety

577
00:28:45,940 --> 00:28:47,920
record exceeded expectations.

578
00:28:48,020 --> 00:28:51,380
A total of just 11 lives were lost, fewer than one third

579
00:28:51,480 --> 00:28:52,680
of the projected fatalities.

580
00:28:56,580 --> 00:28:59,360
As the project continued, some of the lives

581
00:28:59,361 --> 00:29:02,700
most death-defying high-wire acts were yet to come.

582
00:29:06,620 --> 00:29:09,500
Since opening in 1937, the Golden Gate Bridge

583
00:29:09,501 --> 00:29:12,600
has been closed three times because of high winds,

584
00:29:12,601 --> 00:29:15,260
some reaching 75 miles per hour.

585
00:29:15,261 --> 00:29:17,360
The bridge suffered no structural damage.

586
00:29:24,360 --> 00:29:28,100
From this lofty perspective, the Golden Gate Bridge's cables

587
00:29:28,101 --> 00:29:30,740
almost delicate, the way they gracefully drape over

588
00:29:30,840 --> 00:29:32,440
the two towers.

589
00:29:32,540 --> 00:29:36,940
But in reality, the 7,650-foot-long main cables

590
00:29:37,040 --> 00:29:40,540
have to support the massive weight of the suspension spans.

591
00:29:40,640 --> 00:29:43,320
The cable looks like one large wire.

592
00:29:43,420 --> 00:29:47,360
It's three feet in diameter, but it's actually 27,572

593
00:29:47,460 --> 00:29:50,660
individual wires, each the size of a large pencil lead,

594
00:29:50,760 --> 00:29:51,660
if you wish.

595
00:29:51,760 --> 00:29:53,260
Those are put into bundles.

596
00:29:53,360 --> 00:29:54,760
There's 61 bundles.

597
00:29:54,860 --> 00:29:58,100
Each bundle has a little over a million pounds of force on it.

598
00:29:58,101 --> 00:30:02,580
Seven decades ago, as the bridge was under construction,

599
00:30:02,680 --> 00:30:04,540
workers took on the titanic challenge

600
00:30:04,640 --> 00:30:06,840
of crafting the main cables.

601
00:30:06,940 --> 00:30:10,380
More than 80,000 miles of steel wire were forged

602
00:30:10,480 --> 00:30:13,120
and shipped to the bridge site.

603
00:30:13,220 --> 00:30:15,860
The wire was specially designed for the Golden Gate Bridge

604
00:30:15,960 --> 00:30:21,360
from carbon and alloy steel that met exacting specifications.

605
00:30:21,460 --> 00:30:24,420
Samples from each roll were tested for elongation

606
00:30:24,520 --> 00:30:25,560
and strength.

607
00:30:25,561 --> 00:30:28,600
The workers' goal would be to combine these individual wires

608
00:30:28,700 --> 00:30:31,240
to form the main cables.

609
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:32,600
When they came to the end of the spool,

610
00:30:32,700 --> 00:30:34,040
they installed splices.

611
00:30:34,140 --> 00:30:36,200
And the splices were just a little compression fitting,

612
00:30:36,300 --> 00:30:38,000
where they put two pieces of wire together

613
00:30:38,100 --> 00:30:40,700
with a sleeve around them, and they were crimped together.

614
00:30:40,800 --> 00:30:42,980
The splices are stronger than the actual wire, though.

615
00:30:43,080 --> 00:30:45,380
They tested them periodically as part of the quality control

616
00:30:45,480 --> 00:30:46,540
plan here at the bridge.

617
00:30:46,640 --> 00:30:51,080
There are 118,000 splices in these two cables.

618
00:30:51,180 --> 00:30:54,280
In the summer of 1935, workers

619
00:30:54,281 --> 00:30:57,120
workers began the mammoth task of suspending the bridge's

620
00:30:57,220 --> 00:30:59,220
cables.

621
00:30:59,320 --> 00:31:02,820
Initially, shipping lanes were closed so that the first strands

622
00:31:02,920 --> 00:31:06,720
could be dragged across the gate by Coast Guard vessels.

623
00:31:06,820 --> 00:31:09,720
The operation was choreographed by radio operators, who

624
00:31:09,820 --> 00:31:13,840
directed the pilots through the treacherous waters.

625
00:31:13,940 --> 00:31:17,780
Cranes lifted the first small strands to the top.

626
00:31:17,880 --> 00:31:21,080
Once they were in place, a mid-span work platform

627
00:31:21,180 --> 00:31:23,740
was lowered across the gap.

628
00:31:23,741 --> 00:31:26,640
The operation was not a complete success.

629
00:31:26,740 --> 00:31:29,880
The platform became stuck on the initial strands.

630
00:31:29,980 --> 00:31:33,180
Two bridge men volunteered to crawl across the swaying span

631
00:31:33,280 --> 00:31:36,280
to free the platform.

632
00:31:36,380 --> 00:31:38,220
In the vernacular of the bridge man,

633
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:41,500
this was called swimming the cable.

634
00:31:41,600 --> 00:31:44,800
Once this hazardous task was complete,

635
00:31:44,900 --> 00:31:47,300
workers could begin the elaborate process of bundling

636
00:31:47,400 --> 00:31:49,640
and draping the mighty cables over the steel tower

637
00:31:49,740 --> 00:31:52,600
superstructures.

638
00:31:52,601 --> 00:31:56,080
This challenge had to be met at heights up to 746 feet

639
00:31:56,180 --> 00:31:59,180
above the water surface, and sometimes in brutal wind

640
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:03,620
conditions exceeding 45 miles an hour.

641
00:32:03,720 --> 00:32:05,920
On either shore, the wires connected

642
00:32:06,020 --> 00:32:10,620
to massive adjusting rods, while 150-ton saddles over the tops

643
00:32:10,720 --> 00:32:13,980
of the towers cradled the cable in place.

644
00:32:14,080 --> 00:32:19,160
A network of spinning wheel carriages transported 27,572

645
00:32:19,161 --> 00:32:23,500
across the span, six wires at a time.

646
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:25,960
Workers placed individual wires by hand

647
00:32:26,060 --> 00:32:28,200
into a geometric pattern.

648
00:32:28,300 --> 00:32:30,900
Then radio operators transmitted instructions

649
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:34,500
to the opposing bases to tighten the stretch.

650
00:32:34,600 --> 00:32:37,640
Enormous reels of fresh wire were woven into the bridge

651
00:32:37,740 --> 00:32:40,040
with incredible speed.

652
00:32:40,140 --> 00:32:42,820
During one record-breaking eight-hour shift,

653
00:32:42,920 --> 00:32:47,560
1,000 miles of wire were spun over the gate.

654
00:32:47,561 --> 00:32:50,060
This spirit of accomplishment allowed the suspension

655
00:32:50,160 --> 00:32:55,360
of the cables to be completed in only 191 days.

656
00:32:55,460 --> 00:32:58,200
But as the men cheered the arrival of the last strand

657
00:32:58,300 --> 00:33:04,720
on May 20, 1936, they knew the job was far from over.

658
00:33:04,820 --> 00:33:08,820
Before the cables were complete, 3,000 wooden pallets were

659
00:33:08,920 --> 00:33:13,220
attached, creating a swaying catwalk over the bay.

660
00:33:13,320 --> 00:33:15,920
From here, workers could supervise the last stage

661
00:33:15,921 --> 00:33:19,920
of the weaving, the compacting of the wire bundles.

662
00:33:20,020 --> 00:33:23,520
First by hand, then with the help of hydraulic machinery,

663
00:33:23,620 --> 00:33:26,260
the thousands of wires were bonded together

664
00:33:26,360 --> 00:33:29,760
and then wrapped in a tight cocoon of steel thread.

665
00:33:29,860 --> 00:33:31,860
Every millimeter of the final cable

666
00:33:31,960 --> 00:33:34,980
was manicured by powerful metal fingers

667
00:33:35,080 --> 00:33:39,580
and then painted to assure a watertight seal.

668
00:33:39,680 --> 00:33:44,320
The result was twin cables exactly three feet in diameter.

669
00:33:44,321 --> 00:33:47,660
From these, the roadway would be suspended with vertical cables,

670
00:33:47,760 --> 00:33:53,660
then being manufactured nearly 3,000 miles away to the east.

671
00:33:53,760 --> 00:33:57,000
The suspenders dropped to attach a level stretch of steel

672
00:33:57,100 --> 00:34:01,540
trusses and roadway panels along horizontal beams 25 feet

673
00:34:01,640 --> 00:34:06,880
apart, 90 feet long, and 8 and 1⁄2 feet deep.

674
00:34:06,980 --> 00:34:10,340
Bridgemen rode the enormous steel supports into position

675
00:34:10,440 --> 00:34:14,320
as the 1 and 1⁄2-mile track blossomed over the bay.

676
00:34:14,321 --> 00:34:18,320
Twisted webs of steel rods were laid across the width of the

677
00:34:18,420 --> 00:34:23,060
bridge, welded together, then bathed in tons of concrete

678
00:34:23,160 --> 00:34:26,560
delivered by narrow gauge locomotives.

679
00:34:26,660 --> 00:34:28,760
Giant leveling machines ground the surface

680
00:34:28,860 --> 00:34:32,780
to a seamless ribbon of roadway, and painters marked the lanes

681
00:34:32,880 --> 00:34:35,880
where traffic would soon flow.

682
00:34:35,980 --> 00:34:38,980
As construction on the bridge was about to end,

683
00:34:39,080 --> 00:34:41,580
many of the men who were working their ways down the length

684
00:34:41,581 --> 00:34:44,780
down the length of the cables, brought along their lunch

685
00:34:44,880 --> 00:34:48,320
for one last leisurely meal atop the bridge.

686
00:34:48,420 --> 00:34:51,020
They could take pride in what had been accomplished,

687
00:34:51,120 --> 00:34:53,720
and in themselves as exceptional craftsmen.

688
00:34:58,540 --> 00:35:03,440
On May 27, 1937, week-long bridge opening festivities

689
00:35:03,540 --> 00:35:06,980
began with the driving of a ceremonial golden rivet

690
00:35:07,080 --> 00:35:09,080
at mid-span.

691
00:35:09,081 --> 00:35:12,820
It was hammered into place by Edwin Iron Horse Stanley,

692
00:35:12,920 --> 00:35:15,620
the same man who drove the very first rivet on the bridge

693
00:35:15,720 --> 00:35:18,020
four years earlier.

694
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:20,760
But then the multitudes clamored to make themselves

695
00:35:20,860 --> 00:35:22,160
part of history.

696
00:35:22,260 --> 00:35:25,060
And on pedestrian day, which was the first day

697
00:35:25,160 --> 00:35:28,700
that the bridge was open, we had about 30,000 people show up

698
00:35:28,800 --> 00:35:30,660
and walk across the bridge.

699
00:35:30,760 --> 00:35:31,960
But not all of them walked.

700
00:35:32,060 --> 00:35:34,000
We had two sisters that roller skated.

701
00:35:34,100 --> 00:35:36,100
We have people who are very proud of the fact

702
00:35:36,200 --> 00:35:38,080
they were the first to run across it,

703
00:35:38,081 --> 00:35:40,120
or ride a bike across it.

704
00:35:40,220 --> 00:35:42,480
We had the first horse across the bridge.

705
00:35:42,580 --> 00:35:45,060
That was also the last horse across the bridge,

706
00:35:45,160 --> 00:35:49,300
because we don't allow that to happen.

707
00:35:49,400 --> 00:35:51,520
On the second day of the celebration,

708
00:35:51,620 --> 00:35:53,520
Joseph Strauss supervised the cutting

709
00:35:53,620 --> 00:35:56,600
of a ceremonial steel chain, and cars

710
00:35:56,700 --> 00:36:00,060
were allowed to cross for the first time.

711
00:36:00,160 --> 00:36:01,940
It was a triumphant demonstration

712
00:36:02,040 --> 00:36:04,780
of people celebrating technology's ability

713
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:07,280
to serve humanity's dreams.

714
00:36:07,281 --> 00:36:09,480
The joy was infectious.

715
00:36:09,580 --> 00:36:12,520
Flyovers, marching vans, and parade floats

716
00:36:12,620 --> 00:36:15,280
marked the triumph of a vigorous community lifting

717
00:36:15,380 --> 00:36:19,220
itself from the shackles of the Depression.

718
00:36:19,320 --> 00:36:23,520
As it turned out, the dynamic story of the Golden Gate Bridge

719
00:36:23,620 --> 00:36:24,840
was far from over.

720
00:36:28,440 --> 00:36:30,780
The original toll to cross the Golden Gate Bridge

721
00:36:30,880 --> 00:36:35,580
was 50 cents each way, equivalent to almost $7 today.

722
00:36:35,581 --> 00:36:39,520
The current toll is $5, collected only from southbound traffic.

723
00:36:48,720 --> 00:36:51,820
In San Francisco, the Golden Gate Bridge

724
00:36:51,920 --> 00:36:55,640
stands as a permanent reminder of one exceptional moment

725
00:36:55,740 --> 00:36:59,340
when dreams and determination fell into alignment,

726
00:36:59,440 --> 00:37:03,580
a moment that's celebrated with each passing car.

727
00:37:03,581 --> 00:37:08,460
As of 2005, it's estimated that over 1.8 billion vehicles

728
00:37:08,560 --> 00:37:12,060
have traveled across the bridge.

729
00:37:12,160 --> 00:37:14,320
From a practical standpoint, this also

730
00:37:14,420 --> 00:37:17,620
makes this toll bridge an incredible economic success

731
00:37:17,720 --> 00:37:19,120
story.

732
00:37:19,220 --> 00:37:22,860
In total, we had a $35 million construction project

733
00:37:22,960 --> 00:37:27,200
and then another $39 million in financing charges.

734
00:37:27,300 --> 00:37:30,200
That was all completely paid off by 1971.

735
00:37:30,300 --> 00:37:32,780
And as of now, people do not any longer

736
00:37:32,781 --> 00:37:35,120
have a lien on their personal property

737
00:37:35,220 --> 00:37:39,420
to help pay for the construction of the bridge.

738
00:37:39,520 --> 00:37:41,020
For more than three decades,

739
00:37:41,120 --> 00:37:43,520
tolls have been paying for the rising costs of the bridge's

740
00:37:43,620 --> 00:37:46,520
administration and maintenance.

741
00:37:46,620 --> 00:37:48,820
We have about 110 people at the Golden Gate Bridge

742
00:37:48,920 --> 00:37:51,440
involved full time in bridge maintenance.

743
00:37:51,540 --> 00:37:53,940
And that includes iron workers, painters,

744
00:37:54,040 --> 00:37:57,180
and a whole bunch of allied crafts, operating engineers

745
00:37:57,280 --> 00:37:58,540
and others.

746
00:37:58,640 --> 00:38:00,340
We're really a self-contained operation here.

747
00:38:00,440 --> 00:38:02,780
If we have to replace some steel element of the bridge,

748
00:38:02,781 --> 00:38:05,540
it would be manufactured right here at the bridge.

749
00:38:05,541 --> 00:38:09,620
We have a full fabrication shop and almost all the parts we

750
00:38:09,621 --> 00:38:10,780
can build right here.

751
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:16,540
In recent decades, two major structural improvements

752
00:38:16,541 --> 00:38:18,120
have ensured the bridge's longevity.

753
00:38:20,820 --> 00:38:24,540
In the 1970s, all of the suspender ropes that hang off

754
00:38:24,541 --> 00:38:29,280
the main cables were replaced after showing signs of corrosion.

755
00:38:29,281 --> 00:38:33,360
In the 1980s, the reinforced concrete road deck was replaced

756
00:38:33,460 --> 00:38:36,120
with a steel deck.

757
00:38:36,220 --> 00:38:40,400
So what you drive on today is just a relatively thin steel

758
00:38:40,500 --> 00:38:43,720
plate with some ribs underneath that give it its stiffness with

759
00:38:43,820 --> 00:38:47,960
a very thin layer of asphalt on top.

760
00:38:48,060 --> 00:38:50,940
The steel deck is quite a bit lighter than the concrete deck

761
00:38:51,040 --> 00:38:54,040
was, and so when the steel deck was put on, the bridge actually

762
00:38:54,140 --> 00:38:57,600
rose up several feet.

763
00:38:57,601 --> 00:38:59,760
More recent changes to the bridge were compelled

764
00:38:59,860 --> 00:39:02,900
by another concern, security.

765
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:05,400
In the post-911 world, as caretakers

766
00:39:05,500 --> 00:39:08,640
of an international icon like the Golden Gate Bridge,

767
00:39:08,740 --> 00:39:12,040
I don't think anyone can deny the fact that we might be

768
00:39:12,140 --> 00:39:13,840
a target to someone.

769
00:39:13,940 --> 00:39:18,320
We started enhancing security here back when the first World

770
00:39:18,420 --> 00:39:21,100
Trade Center bombing took place in the early 90s.

771
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:23,000
And certainly after September 11th,

772
00:39:23,100 --> 00:39:25,660
we have added people, we've added systems.

773
00:39:25,661 --> 00:39:29,460
It is a very safe place to be.

774
00:39:29,461 --> 00:39:32,000
We've got more cameras, we've got more alarms,

775
00:39:32,001 --> 00:39:35,000
we've got more personnel, and we're deploying them

776
00:39:35,001 --> 00:39:37,000
in different ways than we ever have in the past.

777
00:39:37,001 --> 00:39:40,000
We actually operate as if we're on an orange alert now.

778
00:39:40,001 --> 00:39:44,480
We've been elevated for the last three or four years.

779
00:39:44,481 --> 00:39:45,940
Rather than going back and forth,

780
00:39:45,941 --> 00:39:48,920
we just stay at what essentially is an orange alert.

781
00:39:48,921 --> 00:39:51,260
The only change we would make is if we ever had to go

782
00:39:51,261 --> 00:39:55,660
to red alert, we do have plans we implement at that time.

783
00:39:55,661 --> 00:39:58,960
Ironically, the one event that posed the biggest test of the

784
00:39:59,060 --> 00:40:03,460
bridge's structural integrity wasn't an attempt to destroy it,

785
00:40:03,560 --> 00:40:08,200
but rather to celebrate it, the bridge's 50th anniversary in

786
00:40:08,300 --> 00:40:09,700
1987.

787
00:40:09,800 --> 00:40:12,640
The 50th anniversary was a wonderful

788
00:40:12,740 --> 00:40:13,680
experience.

789
00:40:13,780 --> 00:40:18,020
The weather was fine, although we had planned for 40,000 to

790
00:40:18,120 --> 00:40:22,820
50,000 people, it was closer to 250,000 to 275,000 people at

791
00:40:22,821 --> 00:40:24,820
at any one time out there on the bridge.

792
00:40:24,821 --> 00:40:27,820
We had to stop transit at 515 in the morning

793
00:40:27,821 --> 00:40:29,820
because we couldn't accommodate any more people.

794
00:40:29,821 --> 00:40:32,820
We had both the south and north approaches jammed wall to wall

795
00:40:32,821 --> 00:40:33,820
with people.

796
00:40:33,821 --> 00:40:38,820
34C, would you please circle the Presidio area.

797
00:40:38,821 --> 00:40:44,820
Our problem is the influx of people still coming in.

798
00:40:44,821 --> 00:40:46,820
What was interesting is that's the greatest loading

799
00:40:46,821 --> 00:40:47,820
the bridge has ever seen.

800
00:40:47,821 --> 00:40:50,820
It carries trucks and buses and cars every day.

801
00:40:50,821 --> 00:40:55,160
every day, but people packed in there, like sardines in a can,

802
00:40:55,161 --> 00:40:57,620
actually caused the bridge to sag.

803
00:40:57,621 --> 00:40:59,660
The bridge is designed to move up and down.

804
00:40:59,661 --> 00:41:02,360
It could go down up to 10 feet and up to 6 feet,

805
00:41:02,361 --> 00:41:04,660
depending on temperature and loading.

806
00:41:04,661 --> 00:41:06,160
This tested it that day.

807
00:41:06,161 --> 00:41:08,780
The bridge sagged many feet, and it was visible.

808
00:41:08,781 --> 00:41:10,780
And some of the suspenders went slack.

809
00:41:10,781 --> 00:41:12,780
But it did not overstress the bridge that day,

810
00:41:12,781 --> 00:41:15,080
and there was no damage.

811
00:41:15,081 --> 00:41:16,980
The bridge withstood the tremendous weight

812
00:41:16,981 --> 00:41:19,380
of the celebrating throng.

813
00:41:19,381 --> 00:41:22,020
This convinced experts that it was likely to stand firm

814
00:41:22,120 --> 00:41:26,820
through most natural or unnatural calamities.

815
00:41:26,920 --> 00:41:30,760
But then in 1989, the region was rocked by the shock waves

816
00:41:30,860 --> 00:41:33,460
radiating from the Loma Prieta earthquake,

817
00:41:33,560 --> 00:41:37,660
centered 56 miles to the south.

818
00:41:37,760 --> 00:41:40,540
Even though the Golden Gate Bridge persevered,

819
00:41:40,640 --> 00:41:43,300
the city suffered widespread damage,

820
00:41:43,400 --> 00:41:47,600
and roadway sections collapsed on the nearby Bay Bridge.

821
00:41:47,601 --> 00:41:50,440
Since the Golden Gate Bridge is situated between two major

822
00:41:50,540 --> 00:41:53,440
earthquake faults, the Hayward Fault and the San Andreas

823
00:41:53,540 --> 00:41:57,840
Fault, engineers decided the time had come to make changes.

824
00:41:57,940 --> 00:42:00,720
Today, we know more about earthquakes and seismic

825
00:42:00,820 --> 00:42:02,920
engineering than they did in the 1930s.

826
00:42:03,020 --> 00:42:05,380
So we have a seismic retrofit program underway here at the

827
00:42:05,480 --> 00:42:06,680
Golden Gate Bridge.

828
00:42:06,780 --> 00:42:09,060
Nothing we're doing today detracts from what they did

829
00:42:09,160 --> 00:42:10,820
before us.

830
00:42:10,920 --> 00:42:13,620
Quite frankly, they didn't know a whole lot about the mechanics

831
00:42:13,720 --> 00:42:15,720
of earthquakes, about the mechanics of ground motion,

832
00:42:15,721 --> 00:42:17,720
of ground motion and those kinds of things.

833
00:42:17,721 --> 00:42:20,720
And one of the things is that today we know an awful lot more

834
00:42:20,721 --> 00:42:22,720
about how the ground moves and how structures

835
00:42:22,721 --> 00:42:24,720
like the Golden Gate Bridge respond.

836
00:42:24,721 --> 00:42:27,720
And so with computer simulation and tools that they didn't have

837
00:42:27,721 --> 00:42:29,720
available when they designed the bridge,

838
00:42:29,721 --> 00:42:31,720
we're able to make the bridge an awful lot stronger

839
00:42:31,721 --> 00:42:34,720
without adding a whole lot of steel.

840
00:42:34,721 --> 00:42:37,720
We're adding seismic energy dissipating devices to the arch

841
00:42:37,721 --> 00:42:40,720
so it doesn't bang into the concrete pylons.

842
00:42:40,721 --> 00:42:43,720
Also, we're replacing the steel towers that exist on land

843
00:42:43,721 --> 00:42:44,720
behind us here.

844
00:42:44,721 --> 00:42:47,620
Those great towers you see are new steel towers.

845
00:42:47,720 --> 00:42:50,460
We constructed temporary towers or temporary supports

846
00:42:50,560 --> 00:42:53,120
just outside of each tower, and one at a time,

847
00:42:53,220 --> 00:42:56,460
we would jack up the bridge, we would cut out the old towers,

848
00:42:56,560 --> 00:43:00,060
and install new steel towers that look similar to the old towers,

849
00:43:00,160 --> 00:43:01,740
but they're much stronger.

850
00:43:01,840 --> 00:43:03,600
They use modern ductile steel.

851
00:43:03,700 --> 00:43:06,380
They use modern details, modern high strength bolts

852
00:43:06,480 --> 00:43:07,480
instead of rivets.

853
00:43:09,540 --> 00:43:11,380
As engineers complete the bridge's

854
00:43:11,381 --> 00:43:15,380
the bridge's seismic retrofit, they are exercising great care

855
00:43:15,381 --> 00:43:19,380
to ensure that the changes to the bridge are undetectable

856
00:43:19,381 --> 00:43:24,380
and match the original architectural style.

857
00:43:24,381 --> 00:43:27,380
When completed, the retrofit should allow the bridge to

858
00:43:27,381 --> 00:43:31,380
withstand an earthquake as great as the 1906 disaster that

859
00:43:31,381 --> 00:43:36,380
brought the city to its knees. This, along with the bridge's

860
00:43:36,381 --> 00:43:39,380
attentive maintenance and vigilant security, promises that

861
00:43:39,381 --> 00:43:42,040
that it will endure as one of the treasured icons of the

862
00:43:42,041 --> 00:43:46,220
American landscape. I don't think anyone knows

863
00:43:46,221 --> 00:43:48,820
exactly how long a bridge like this is going to last.

864
00:43:48,821 --> 00:43:51,520
The parts of the bridge that can be replaced have been

865
00:43:51,521 --> 00:43:53,720
replaced, and other parts of the bridge just have to be

866
00:43:53,721 --> 00:43:55,940
maintained. But with proper maintenance,

867
00:43:55,941 --> 00:43:58,240
the bridge should last essentially forever.

868
00:44:01,740 --> 00:44:04,380
Today, it's difficult to imagine this turbulent

869
00:44:04,381 --> 00:44:07,780
stretch of sea without the Golden Gate Bridge linking the

870
00:44:07,781 --> 00:44:11,540
the rocky shorelines, but it represents much more than an

871
00:44:11,541 --> 00:44:14,180
impressive engineering achievement.

872
00:44:14,181 --> 00:44:18,420
It stands as a tribute to the concept of community, the daring

873
00:44:18,421 --> 00:44:23,700
of those who erected it, and the belief that all things are

874
00:44:23,701 --> 00:44:25,700
possible.

